            "text": "Yes — four layers, and your reasoning is exactly right. Each fold doubles the layer count: after the first fold there are 2 layers, and folding that in half again doubles it to 4.\n\nOne thing worth noticing about *why* it doubles: folding doesn't just stack paper on paper — the crease region stays connected, so each fold creates a hinge. After two folds, the strip has four thicknesses across its length but only two creases, and one end is the original loose end while the other is the folded edge.\n\nSo how would you describe the result after a third fold?"
